  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agriculture and WASH Drought Anticipatory Actions in Goro Dola Woreda, East Borena</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtThe project focuses on mitigating the impacts of anticipated below normal rains during the October-December season in Goro Dola Woreda, East Borena Zone, Oromia Region. It addresses critical vulnerabilities in water access, agriculture, and livestock, which are expected to worsen due to prolonged drought conditions. The intervention aims to strengthen agricultural resilience, improve water infrastructure, and promote climate-adaptive farming practices to ensure community livelihoods are protected.ltbrgtThe project will be implemented over 7 months, from October 28, 2024, to May 27, 2025, with a total budget of $499,999.02. It will target 93,011&#xA0;individuals, including 4,565 internally displaced people and&#xA0;88,446&#xA0;members of host communities. The focus sectors include agriculture (55% of the budget) and WASH (45% of the budget).ltbrgtKey agriculture interventions include livestock mass vaccinations and voucher based treatment, MPC, climate smart&#xA0;vegetable,&#xA0;crop and fodder&#xA0;seeds&#xA0;support to protect the local economy including&#xA0;training sessions and EWS.&#xA0;Eighteen sessions will engage local communities and stakeholders in preparedness planning to help them anticipate and mitigate the effects of drought.ltbrgtTo address critical water shortages, the project focuses on pond and water point rehabilitation, water harvesting, and water line extensions. The initiative aligns with ongoing national and local efforts in agriculture, WASH, and disaster preparedness, ensuring a coordinated and integrated response.ltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
